    "available_at": "2026-11-12T14:06:40.134604+00:00",
    "body": "Seven H.B. Fuller manufacturing sites in North America and Europe will shut down as the adhesives maker moves production into fewer plants. The company announced the closures earlier this week and said some work is moving to a new aerospace manufacturing site in Charlotte, North Carolina, and to a plant that recently began operating in Monterrey, Mexico .\nWhat’s Related\nThe closures are part of a plan H.B. Fuller outlined in 2025 to consolidate manufacturing and distribution. The company had 64 manufacturing sites worldwide at the end of its fiscal third quarter, down from 82 at the end of 2024. It expects to be down to 55 by 2030.\n“We have moved decisively from planning to execution, and these results validate the opportunity to increase efficiency, reduce operating costs, and better serve our customers,” said Celeste Mastin, H.B. Fuller’s president and CEO. The company did not name the seven sites, give individual closure dates, or say how many jobs would be affected.\nWhere the work is going\nH.B. Fuller said in May that it expected the Charlotte facility to open in early 2027. The plant will bring aerospace adhesive production, packaging , testing and quality checks under one roof. It will have specialized mixing equipment and labs for developing and testing products.\nThe Monterrey plant is already running. H.B. Fuller did not specify which product lines would move to each location or when the transfers would be complete. The company expects operating costs for production moved to newer facilities to fall by as much as 50%.\nPlans for North America\nH.B. Fuller is also reorganizing North American distribution around six regional centers. It expects those centers to handle about 60% of its North American volume by 2030 and says the change will put products closer to customers.\nThe company expects the number of locations it ships from via less-than-truckload carriers to fall by more than 80%. That means carriers would pick up those shipments from far fewer locations. H.B. Fuller also expects the change to give it more control over inventory and reduce the amount of cash tied up in stock.\n$75 million in annual savings\nH.B. Fuller expects the manufacturing and distribution plan to save $75 million annually by 2030. It expects to reach about $25 million in annualized savings by the end of fiscal 2026 and add another $20 million to $25 million in savings in 2027.\nThe company expects capital spending on the project to fall below $25 million in 2027, after about $100 million in total investment through 2026. It expects spending to decline further after 2027 as more production moves to newer plants that require less investment in maintenance.",
